><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250604144904+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250604144906+10'00'" name="d47e31aa-a350-4942-84b5-b601adb1a168" page="5" coords="310.733795,438.763000,561.504089,438.763000,310.733795,428.037506,561.504089,428.037506,310.733795,426.745483,561.447083,426.745483,310.733795,416.019989,561.447083,416.019989,310.733795,414.784973,561.514526,414.784973,310.733795,404.059509,561.514526,404.059509,310.733795,402.767456,561.466309,402.767456,310.733795,392.041992,561.466309,392.041992,310.733795,390.750000,561.543213,390.750000,310.733795,380.024506,561.543213,380.024506,310.733795,378.789490,561.446960,378.789490,310.733795,368.063995,561.446960,368.063995,310.733795,366.771973,561.503784,366.771973,310.733795,356.046509,561.503784,356.046509,310.733795,354.754456,561.522705,354.754456,310.733795,344.028992,561.522705,344.028992,310.733795,342.794006,561.399475,342.794006,310.733795,332.068512,561.399475,332.068512,310.733795,330.776489,561.409058,330.776489,310.733795,320.050995,561.409058,320.050995,310.733795,318.758972,561.514038,318.758972,310.733795,308.033508,561.514038,308.033508,310.733795,306.798462,561.438477,306.798462,310.733795,296.072998,561.438477,296.072998,310.733795,294.781006,449.566406,294.781006,310.733795,284.055511,449.566406,284.055511" rect="310.398621,283.720337,561.878418,439.098175" subject="Comment on Text" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Mastalgia and exercise-induced breast pain were commonly&#xD;reported by this cohort of elite female athletes, and both types&#xD;of breast pain were perceived to negatively affect the sporting&#xD;performance of these athletes. Exercise-induced breast pain was&#xD;also significantly related to type of activity, with different levels&#xD;of pain reported during fundamental activities such as jumping,&#xD;throwing, kicking and other sport-related movements. Coaches,&#xD;allied health and medical personnel associated with elite female&#xD;sport, as well as the athletes themselves, should be made aware&#xD;of the potential negative impact of breast pain on sporting&#xD;performance. More importantly, evidence-based strategies to&#xD;manage breast pain for elite female athletes need to be developed,&#xD;implemented and evaluated.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="5" rect="609.448975,346.763000,793.448975,438.763000"
/></highlight
